## Rules

1. When an OOV name means the same thing as an entry that already exists
   above, emit an absorption (`candidate_name` -> `canonical_name`) instead
   of a new entry. Absorbing beats adding: two names for one property make
   two documents look incomparable.
2. When several OOV names are synonyms of each other, pick ONE canonical
   lower snake_case name and emit exactly ONE entry whose
   `source_candidates` lists every name it absorbs — including the canonical
   name itself when that name was observed.
3. Every new entry needs a precise `definition`, a `value_type` that is one
   of `number|date|boolean|enum|text`, and a `reason`. When `value_type` is
   `enum`, `enum_values` must cover every observed value for that name.
   Relation entries need a `definition` and a `reason`, plus `domain` and
   `range_` when the examples make the endpoint types clear.
4. Never redefine, rename, or narrow an entry that already exists above.
   This step is additions and absorptions only.
5. Names must be lower snake_case English. Reject transliteration and
   Korean names — rewrite them into snake_case English.
6. Give a `reason` for every absorption and every entry. It becomes the
   audit trail a human reads later, so say what evidence made you decide,
   not that you decided.

Leave a name out entirely when the usage evidence is too thin to define it
honestly. It will come back with more usage next time — a wrong definition
will not.
